---
/**
 * Everyone, Serialized: Futurist No. 2, the companion to Everyone,
 * Searchable. A scrolly in nine chapters, set as an instrument readout that
 * descends: step text on the left, the exhibit pinned on the right and drawn
 * live on canvas; on phones the exhibit pins and the steps slide over it.
 * The page darkens level by level to the floor (IV to VII), surfaces for the
 * hope (VIII), and ends on the iris-dark coda. ECG paper, ink, serial red.
 *
 * Ported from docs/prototypes/everyone-serialized-prototype.html, which is
 * the design of record and ships as approved: its markup, copy and script
 * are the prototype's byte for byte, and its CSS is the prototype's with one
 * class (`.ser`) added to every selector, so its cascade is unchanged. The
 * fonts are self-hosted under the names it loads them by. The only additions
 * are the ones Everyone, Searchable made to its own prototype: the link back
 * to the Gallery at the right end of the top bar, the series label and
 * byline in the kicker, the byline in the coda, the line to /method under
 * the sources, and the shared foot (ScrollFoot) after them. The body's
 * starting data-depth="0" is left to the script, which sets it on its first
 * pass; no rule styles depth 0, so the first paint is the same.
 *
 * Same page system as Everyone, Searchable (Poem.astro), and the writings
 * entry opts the route out of the site's effects (`treatment: scrolly`).
 * Title, description and date come from the gallery entry (src/lib/pieces.ts).
 */
import Poem from '../../layouts/Poem.astro';
import SeriesMark from '../../components/scroll/SeriesMark.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/scroll/Byline.astro';
import ScrollFoot from '../../components/scroll/ScrollFoot.astro';
import MethodLink from '../../components/scroll/MethodLink.astro';
import { series } from '../../config/series';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../config/site';
import { getPiece } from '../../lib/pieces';

const piece = await getPiece('everyone-serialized');
if (!piece.series) throw new Error('everyone-serialized: the gallery entry needs a series.');
const { title, description, date } = piece;
const s = series[piece.series];
---

<Poem
  title={title}
  description={description}
  year={String(date.getUTCFullYear())}
  published={date}
  night="#ebe7e1"
  accent="#c0281c"
  genre="Essay"
  scheme="light"
  exit={false}
  partOf={{ name: s.title, url: `${SITE_URL}${s.href}`, type: 'CreativeWorkSeries' }}
  preload={[
    '/fonts/big-shoulders-stencil-display-latin-wght-normal.woff2',
    '/fonts/newsreader-latin-opsz-normal.woff2',
    '/fonts/courier-prime-latin-400-normal.woff2',
    '/fonts/courier-prime-latin-700-normal.woff2',
  ]}
  bodyClass="ser"
>
<div class="bar" data-twin-skip>
  <details class="toc">
    <summary><span class="chev" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="now">Contents</span></summary>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#the-signature"><span>I</span><span>The Signature</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#demonstrated"><span>II</span><span>Demonstrated</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#standardized"><span>III</span><span>Standardized</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#patented"><span>IV</span><span>Patented</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#imagined"><span>V</span><span>Imagined</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-method"><span>VI</span><span>The Method</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-missing-because"><span>VII</span><span>The Missing Because</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-hope"><span>VIII</span><span>Where the Hope Is</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#their-eyes"><span>IX</span><span>The Color of Their Eyes</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#sources"><span>&#183;</span><span>Sources</span></a></li>
    </ol>
  </details>
  <span class="bar-title">Everyone, Serialized</span>
  <span class="bar-end">
    <span class="gauge" aria-hidden="true"><b>Depth</b><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
    <a class="bar-exit" href="/gallery/" data-og-hide><svg class="bar-frame" viewBox="0 0 14 11" aria-hidden="true"><rect x=".75" y=".75" width="12.5" height="9.5"/><rect class="pic" x="4" y="3.5" width="6" height="4"/></svg><span class="bar-exit-t">Gallery</span></a>
  </span>
  <span class="progress" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</div>

<header class="hero" id="top">
  <p class="kicker"><span><SeriesMark id="everyone-serialized" /></span><span><Byline before="By" /></span><span>November 2026</span></p>
  <h1 class="title"><span>Everyone,</span><span class="second">Serialized</span></h1>
  <p class="dek">Your heartbeat, your walk, the shadow you cast in a radio wave. Each one is becoming a number that travels without you. How deep that goes, where the method came from, and where the hope is.</p>
  <canvas class="pulse" id="pulse" role="img" aria-label="A heartbeat trace runs left to right and turns into a barcode."></canvas>
  <p class="pulse-cap" aria-hidden="true"><span>Signal</span><span>Serial</span></p>
  <blockquote class="epigraph"><p>And David&#8217;s heart smote him after that he had numbered the people.</p><cite>2 Samuel 24:10</cite></blockquote>
  <p class="cue">Scroll to start the descent</p>
</header>

<main>

<!-- I -->
<section class="chapter" id="the-signature" data-chapter data-num="I" data-title="The Signature" data-depth="0">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">I</span><h2>The Signature</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter I of IX</span><span>Surface</span></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="sig" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="Three traces on ECG paper: a heartbeat, a walking gait and a radio shadow. They are sampled into a barcode and a serial number. Then a ladder of four levels appears: demonstrated, standardized, patented, imagined."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>Everyone is about to be serialized. The body already issues the number.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><p>Your heartbeat has a shape. So does your walk, and so does the shadow you cast in a radio wave. Each one holds steady enough to tell you from the next person.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>A serial number is a fact built to travel. It goes places you never go and gets read by people you will never meet.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>This one goes down four levels: what has been demonstrated, what is being standardized, what has been patented, and what has only been imagined.</p><p>Every claim wears its depth.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- II -->
<section class="chapter" id="demonstrated" data-chapter data-num="II" data-title="Demonstrated" data-depth="1" data-level="1">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">II</span><h2>Demonstrated</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter II of IX</span><b>Level 1 of 4</b></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="wifi" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="A room seen from above. A Wi-Fi access point sends waves past a person and reads a heart rate of 72 from the distortion. Two access points match the same body at 95.5 percent. A laser reads a heartbeat from 200 meters. The fine print: one person per room, fourteen subjects, thirty seconds of sitting still. The result is stamped same body, with the name left blank."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><span class="yr">2025 &#183; Santa Cruz</span><p>A Wi-Fi chip that costs five to ten dollars reads a heart rate from across the room. Five seconds of signal. Clinical-grade accuracy.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><span class="yr">2025 &#183; Rome</span><p>Researchers at Sapienza train a network to recognize people by the way their bodies distort Wi-Fi. No camera, no phone in your pocket. It scores 95.5 percent on its benchmark.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><span class="yr">2019 &#183; The Pentagon</span><p>A laser called Jetson reads a cardiac signature through a shirt from 200 meters.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>Now the fine print. One person in the room at a time. A benchmark of fourteen people. Thirty seconds of sitting still, plus a library of heartbeats to match against.</p><p>The demos are real, and they are small.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="5"><div class="card"><p>Every one of them also stops at the same sentence. Same body as before.</p><p>Hold on to that. It matters later.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- III -->
<section class="chapter" id="standardized" data-chapter data-num="III" data-title="Standardized" data-depth="2" data-level="2">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">III</span><h2>Standardized</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter III of IX</span><b>Level 2 of 4</b></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="isac" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="A cell tower sweeps a beam across a skyline. The same signal connects and senses. A drone is bracketed as target one, then people on the street. A storefront is labeled Shopping Experience Tracker, with three carriers feeding one outside firm. A cost meter fills and the scene is stamped draft."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>Level two. The next cellular network is being designed to sense the world as well as connect it. The industry calls this integrated sensing and communication.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><span class="yr">Early 2026</span><p>The standards body finishes its 6G requirements study. A network that can perceive its physical surroundings is on the list of core use cases.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>The first target written into the standard is drones. In surveys of the same work, one of the four use-case families is called human-centric sensing.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>One European research project lists a use case called the Shopping Experience Tracker. An outside company pools sensing results from several carriers at once.</p><p>Nobody leaked that. It is in the paper.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="5"><div class="card"><p>The fine print here is money. Sensing all the time costs compute all the time, and analysts already see the consumer pitch losing its business case.</p><p>The privacy rules are still in draft.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- IV -->
<section class="chapter" id="patented" data-chapter data-num="IV" data-title="Patented" data-depth="3" data-level="3">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">IV</span><h2>Patented</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter IV of IX</span><b>Level 3 of 4</b></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="token" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="An illustration of a patent application, WO 2020/060606, Cryptocurrency System Using Body Activity Data, with its loop of server, device and sensor, verify and award. It is annotated wearables and phones, no implants in the text, US filing abandoned, and stamped blueprint. Then five stages show how a body becomes a token: capture, template, bind, attest, settle. Then an iris scanner issuing a credential and paying tokens. Then a password that rotates beside a heartbeat that never does."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><span class="yr">2020</span><p>Level three. Microsoft publishes a patent application titled <em>Cryptocurrency System Using Body Activity Data</em>.</p><p>Its filing number, WO2020060606, did most of the marketing.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><p>Read the text. A server hands you a task. A sensor in your device reads your body&#8217;s response. If the reading passes, you get paid.</p><p>The sensing is done by wearables and phones. Implants never appear, and the US filing is listed as abandoned.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>So file it correctly. It is a blueprint. Someone drew the loop: task, body, verification, payout.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>Here is how a body becomes a token. Capture a trait. Boil it down to a compact code. Bind the code to a key.</p><p>From then on every interaction can emit a signed claim, and rules can pay or charge on the claims.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="5"><div class="card"><p>A live version exists. World scans an iris, issues a credential, and pays its holders in tokens on a schedule.</p><p>More than twelve million people have looked into the Orb.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="6"><div class="card"><p>Carry one fact up from this level. A password can be rotated.</p><p>A heartbeat can&#8217;t.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- V -->
<section class="chapter" id="imagined" data-chapter data-num="V" data-title="Imagined" data-depth="4" data-level="4">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">V</span><h2>Imagined</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter V of IX</span><b>Level 4 of 4</b></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="dust" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="Hundreds of drifting motes in the dark, with a one millimeter scale bar. A wireframe cube one cubic millimeter in size is labeled 1997, Berkeley to DARPA. Each mote gets a tiny halo for its radio range, and meters for power and range sit nearly empty. Then three tenses: can, is being designed to, has been imagined, with the last one underlined."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>Level four, the floor. Smart dust: sensors the size of a grain of sand, scattered by the thousand.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><span class="yr">1997</span><p>The idea is older than the smartphone. A Berkeley team pitches DARPA on sensor nodes one cubic millimeter in size.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>Nearly thirty years on, the walls are the same two. Power, and antennas. At dust scale a radio reaches a few millimeters.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>&#8220;Everything you touch will carry your signature&#8221; lives down here. It has been imagined, and it has been funded.</p><p>Keep the tense honest on the way back up: can, is being designed to, has been imagined.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- VI -->
<section class="chapter" id="the-method" data-chapter data-num="VI" data-title="The Method" data-depth="3">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">VI</span><h2>The Method</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter VI of IX</span><span>How it was done before</span></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="card" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="A blank punch card. Fields are punched into it one at a time: surname, census, body, print. A deck of cards stacks up behind it under the years 1890, 1933 and 1939. A number field is punched, and the legend For Social Security Purposes, Not For Identification is struck through, dated 1946 to 1972."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>The sensor is new. The method is old, and it has four steps.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><span class="yr">Step one</span><p>Make people legible. Surnames imposed for the tax roll. The census. Paris police filing people by the measurements of their bodies in the 1880s.</p><p>A British magistrate in Bengal taking a handprint on a contract in 1858, then fingerprints from pensioners.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><span class="yr">Step two</span><p>Mechanize the count. Hollerith&#8217;s punch cards tabulated the American census of 1890. Hollerith machines from IBM&#8217;s German subsidiary processed the German censuses of 1933 and 1939.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><span class="yr">Step three</span><p>Promise a narrow purpose. From 1946 to 1972, Social Security cards carried the words &#8220;not for identification.&#8221;</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="loop" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="A loop with four stations: sense, compare, reward, behave. A reading circles it, faster at each step. Older versions are named below: scrip, stopwatch, lever, points, dongle. The loop tightens around a person in the middle, and a door marked care opens onto it."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><span class="yr">Step four</span><p>Close the loop with rewards. Company scrip, good only at the company store. The foreman&#8217;s stopwatch. The lever and the pellet. Loyalty points. The safe-driver discount from the dongle in your car.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><span class="yr">1948</span><p>Norbert Wiener gives the loop a name, cybernetics, and a logic: sense, compare, correct.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>Attach the sensor to a body and the payout to the reading, and the loop closes around a person. The system measures you, pays you to change, and measures again.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>The door it comes in through is always care. Pensions. Retirement. Fraud prevention.</p><p>The Wi-Fi heartbeat paper is pitched for elder care.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- VII -->
<section class="chapter" id="the-missing-because" data-chapter data-num="VII" data-title="The Missing Because" data-depth="3">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">VII</span><h2>The Missing Because</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter VII of IX</span><span>Data without context</span></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="because" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="A heart-rate chart spikes to 124. The what is filled in and the why is blank. Five possible reasons take turns: fear, a first kiss, grief, coffee, the stairs. The number gains decimals. A polygraph chart is stamped deception indicated. Three case cards compare data, story and truth. Four beats follow: an accurate data point, a missing because, a confident story, a real penalty. A bell curve shows the tails charged first."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>A sensor records what happened. It has no field for why.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><p>A heart rate of 124 reads the same for fear, a first kiss, grief, coffee, and a flight of stairs.</p><p>The reason stays in the room.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>Precision raises the stakes. An exact number lends its authority to whatever story gets built on top of it.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>We have a century of practice. The polygraph measures breath, sweat and pulse, and gets read as lying.</p><p>In 1988 Congress barred most private employers from using it.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="5"><div class="card"><span class="yr">2020</span><p>Gainesville, Florida. Zachary McCoy tracks his bike rides with a fitness app. His loops pass a house that gets burgled, and a location warrant makes him a suspect.</p><p>Detroit. Robert Williams is arrested in front of his wife and daughters on a face match that was wrong.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="6"><div class="card"><span class="yr">2021</span><p>The Netherlands. The tax office&#8217;s fraud hunt brands more than 20,000 families as cheats over childcare benefits, often for errors as small as a missing signature.</p><p>The cabinet resigns.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="7"><div class="card"><p>Same four beats every time. An accurate data point, a missing because, a confident story, a real penalty.</p><p>A token is data with the context stripped out on purpose. That is what lets it travel.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="8"><div class="card"><p>And a loop that rewards the reading charges unusual lives first. The sick. The grieving. The night shift. Anyone whose normal sits outside the model&#8217;s.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- VIII -->
<section class="chapter" id="the-hope" data-chapter data-num="VIII" data-title="Where the Hope Is" data-depth="0">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">VIII</span><h2>Where the Hope Is</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter VIII of IX</span><span>Surfacing</span></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="hope" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="Two sightings match as the same body, but the line to the registry stops at a gate marked enrollment. The gate closes and is stamped declined: no registry, no name. A histogram bunches up just past the line where a reward starts. Two columns compare serialized, where one ID threads through every site, with attested, where each site gets a different one-time proof. A draft privacy study sits beside a calendar page for February 2, 2025. A crowded room turns the signal to noise."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>Now the hope, and there is a real amount of it. Start with the sentence every demo stopped at.</p><p>Same body as before.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><p>A signature needs a registry to become a name. Somebody has to enroll you, and enrollment is a decision.</p><p>It can be declined, limited, and outlawed.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>The loop also poisons itself. Economists call it Goodhart&#8217;s law. Put a reward on a measurement and people perform for the sensor, until the reading describes the game and stops describing them.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>The proof can travel while the identifier stays home. The same math that tokenizes a body can prove &#8220;one real human&#8221; or &#8220;over 21&#8221; and leave nothing behind to link.</p><p>Serialized or attested is a wiring choice, and people can still demand the second.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="5"><div class="card"><p>The window is open. The 6G privacy study is still in draft. Europe has banned social scoring, and emotion recognition at work and school, since February 2025.</p><p>Its ban is built on context. The offense is a score that follows you out of the setting where the data was gathered.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="6"><div class="card"><p>And the physics is on your side more than the brochures admit. Bodies are noisy, rooms are crowded, and watching everything all the time is expensive.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section class="moves" aria-labelledby="moves-h">
    <h3 id="moves-h">Seven moves<small>While the rules are in draft</small></h3>
    <ol>
      <li><span class="mn">01</span><p><b>Ask where the registry is.</b> Every time someone wants your face, finger, iris or pulse, ask who keeps the list and for how long.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mn">02</span><p><b>Decline the enrollment you don&#8217;t need.</b> Convenience is the price being offered. Check whether it&#8217;s enough.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mn">03</span><p><b>Take the proof, keep the identifier.</b> Favor systems that confirm one fact about you and forget the rest.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mn">04</span><p><b>Answer the record.</b> When a number gets a say in your life, insist on a person with authority and a way to supply the because.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mn">05</span><p><b>Keep data where it was given.</b> What you told your doctor belongs with your doctor. The scholar Helen Nissenbaum calls this contextual integrity.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mn">06</span><p><b>Watch the door marked care.</b> The benefit is usually real. Read what else comes through with it.</p></li>
      <li><span class="mn">07</span><p><b>Extend the grace you&#8217;ll need.</b> Hold a data point about someone without finishing their story.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</section>

<!-- IX -->
<section class="chapter" id="their-eyes" data-chapter data-num="IX" data-title="The Color of Their Eyes" data-depth="0">
  <header class="chap"><span class="chap-n">IX</span><h2>The Color of Their Eyes</h2><p class="chap-of"><span>Chapter IX of IX</span><span>Unserialized</span></p></header>
  <div class="scene" data-scene="eyes" data-step="1">
    <div class="scene-in">
      <div class="graphic"><figure class="exhibit"><canvas role="img" aria-label="An iris drawn as rings of black and white code, with a scanning line. The code gives way to a hazel eye in full color. Eight more eyes appear around it, each a different color, and each is labeled with who it belongs to: mom, the barber, coach, your brother, you, next door, the new guy, an old friend, your kid."></canvas></figure></div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step is-active" data-step="1"><div class="card"><p>A machine can read your iris and return a code. It was built to prove you&#8217;re human.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="2"><div class="card"><p>Your neighbor knows the color of your eyes.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="3"><div class="card"><p>A serial number indexes a body. The person is everything the index leaves out: what you pay attention to, what you mean, who you would show up for.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="4"><div class="card"><p>Everyone already runs a context-free reader in their own head. We finish each other&#8217;s stories from a glimpse.</p><p>The machine does at scale what we do to each other daily.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="5"><div class="card"><p>So practice the reverse while it still counts as practice. Hold the data point. Ask for the because. Extend the grace you will need.</p></div></div>
        <div class="step" data-step="6"><div class="card"><p>Know your people by name before a network knows them by number.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</main>

